Toward a Mathematical Theory of Human-Relative Dominance in Superintelligence

Task Spaces, Margin Distributions, and the Geometry of Superhuman Performance

Ouadi Maakoul & SI TEAM


Abstract

The concept of superintelligence remains one of the most consequential and least formally specified constructs in contemporary artificial intelligence research. Existing definitions, from Bostrom's philosophical characterization to Legg and Hutter's universal intelligence measure, capture essential intuitions but leave critical quantities mathematically underdetermined. This paper develops a formal framework in which superintelligence is characterized as human-relative stochastic dominance of capability margins over a measurable task space. We introduce a structured task space with topological and measure-theoretic structure, a normalization axiom for cross-task comparability, a committed optimal-transport cost construction, and a dominance survival function \Theta_A(\lambda) defined as the \mu-measure of tasks on which the agent's margin over a human reference quantile is at least \lambda. We prove measurability of the margin field (Lemma 4.0), establish that the pair (G_A, P_A) of generality and performance depth does not determine the dominance region (Proposition 4.2), and prove that no scalar order embedding of the five-dimensional product order into (\mathbb{R}, \leq) exists (Theorem 5.1). Robustness is formalized via optimal-transport ambiguity sets. Learning efficiency uses a logarithmic acquisition-time ratio. Autonomy is retained as a graded profile (A_0, A_1, A_2, A_3) rather than scalarized, to avoid reintroducing the arbitrariness that Theorem 5.1 prohibits. A reconstruction program via empirical process theory (DKW inequality, Glivenko–Cantelli) establishes finite-sample estimability. A concrete three-domain empirical instantiation is proposed. The collision audit against performance profiles, empirical attainment functions, and stochastic dominance confirms that the human-relative margin construction has no direct precedent.

Keywords: superintelligence, artificial general intelligence, human-relative dominance, stochastic dominance, task-space coverage, optimal transport, learning efficiency, autonomy, empirical process theory, formal epistemology of AI

---

1. Introduction

1.1. The Central Question

The term superintelligence has become one of the most consequential and least formally specified constructs in contemporary discussions of artificial intelligence. It appears in technical research, policy documents, forecasting, and philosophical analysis, yet its mathematical content remains underdetermined.

This dissertation addresses a narrower question:

\boxed{
\textit{Can superintelligence be defined as a measurable human-relative dominance relation over a structured task space, extended by learning efficiency, robustness, and autonomy?}
}

The objective is not to introduce another informal definition of intelligence, but to construct a mathematical framework in which claims of superhuman performance can be stated, compared, and falsified.

1.2. The Fundamental Object

The central mathematical object of the theory is the dominance survival function:

\boxed{
\Theta_A(\lambda) = \mu\left(\left\{T \in \mathcal{T} : M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) \geq \lambda\right\}\right),
}

where:

· \mathcal{T} is a structured task space;
· \mu is a probability measure on \mathcal{T};
· M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) is the superhuman margin of agent A over human reference quantile q on task T;
· \lambda is the intensity of superhuman performance;
· \Theta_A(\lambda) is the extent of superhuman performance at intensity \lambda.

This function encodes the full trade-off between depth and breadth of superhuman capability. All scalar summaries — including generality, performance depth, and any weighted combination — are derived functionals of \Theta_A.

1.3. Methodological Position

This dissertation adopts a strict prior-art principle:

\boxed{
\text{No novelty claim without systematic collision audit.}
}

The collision audit (Section 6) confirms that while the mathematical pattern of survival/coverage functions over task collections is established in performance profiles and empirical attainment functions, the specific construction

\Theta_A(\lambda) = \mu\left[\, p_A(T) - p_H^{(q)}(T) \geq \lambda \,\right]

— where the reference is human-relative, the threshold is a performance margin, and \mu is a measure over a structured cognitive task space — has no direct precedent.

