3. The Structured Task Space

3.1. Definition of a Task

Definition 3.1 (Task). A task is a tuple

T = (\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{C}, \tau, \nu),

where \mathcal{X} is the observation space, \mathcal{Y} the action space, \mathcal{E} the environment, \mathcal{R} the utility functional, \mathcal{C} the resource constraint, \tau the horizon, and \nu the novelty condition.

3.2. The Task Space

Definition 3.2 (Structured Task Space). The task space is a tuple

\mathfrak{T} = (\mathcal{T}, \tau_{\mathcal{T}}, \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}, \mu),

where \mathcal{T} is a set of admissible tasks, \tau_{\mathcal{T}} is a topology on \mathcal{T}, \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}} is a \sigma-algebra containing \tau_{\mathcal{T}}, and \mu: \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}} \to [0,1] is a probability measure with \mu(\mathcal{T}) = 1.

Axiom T1 (Non-degeneracy). For every non-empty open set U \in \tau_{\mathcal{T}}, \mu(U) > 0.

Axiom T2 (Representation invariance). Equivalent representations of the same cognitive task induce the same measure.

Axiom T3 (Difficulty sensitivity). The measure admits explicit representation of task difficulty.

Axiom T4 (Domain coverage). The measure assigns nonzero mass to each major cognitive domain.

Axiom T5 (Stability). Small admissible changes in the task-generation protocol do not cause arbitrarily large changes in the dominance classification.

3.3. Task Cost — Committed Construction

Definition 3.3 (Task Cost). We commit to the following composite cost construction:

\boxed{
c(T, T') = \alpha \cdot d_{\mathcal{X}}(T, T') + \beta \cdot d_{\mathcal{Y}}(T, T') + \gamma \cdot d_{\mathcal{R}}(T, T'),
}

where:

· d_{\mathcal{X}} is a metric on the observation spaces (e.g., a representation-embedding distance);
· d_{\mathcal{Y}} is a metric on the action spaces (e.g., a divergence between action distributions under a fixed reference policy class);
· d_{\mathcal{R}} is the difference in optimal policies under a fixed reference agent class \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{ref}}:

d_{\mathcal{R}}(T, T') = \sup_{\mathcal{A} \in \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{ref}}} \left\|\pi^*_{\mathcal{A}}(T) - \pi^*_{\mathcal{A}}(T')\right\|,

where \pi^*_{\mathcal{A}}(T) is the optimal policy of \mathcal{A} on T;

· \alpha, \beta, \gamma > 0 are weights.

Assumption 3.3a (Regularity). The cost c is symmetric, lower semi-continuous with respect to \tau_{\mathcal{T}} \times \tau_{\mathcal{T}}, vanishes on the diagonal, and satisfies c(T, T') > 0 for T \neq T'.

Under Assumption 3.3a, the c-Wasserstein distance W_c is well-defined.

Remark 3.3. The reference class \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{ref}} must be fixed. For the robustness theorem (Theorem 7.1), we use \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{ref}} consisting of a fixed, finite family of standard architectures. This makes c architecture-dependent at the reference level, but the dependence is explicit and stated.

3.4. Human Reference

Definition 3.4 (Human Reference). For each task T \in \mathcal{T}, let P_T be a task-conditioned human population, with performance distribution F_{P_T}. The human reference quantile of order q \in (0,1) is

p_H^{(q)}(T) = F_{P_T}^{-1}(q).

Remark 3.4 (Directionality and Censoring). For lower-is-better tasks, the sign is reversed. For ceiling effects where the q-quantile is degenerate, alternative constructions (expert panels, pairwise comparisons) are required.

3.5. Normalization Axiom

Axiom N (Normalization compatibility). A normalization is a family of maps \{\phi_T\}_{T \in \mathcal{T}}, with \phi_T: \mathbb{R} \to [0,1], such that for every T:

(N1) \phi_T is monotone (non-decreasing for higher-is-better tasks, non-increasing for lower-is-better tasks);

(N2) \phi_T preserves quantile ordering: if s_H^{(q)} is the q-quantile of raw human scores on T, then \phi_T(s_H^{(q)}) = q;

(N3) \phi_T is continuous.

Under Axiom N, the normalized human reference satisfies p_H^{(q)}(T) = q. Therefore the margin

M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) = \phi_T(s_A(T)) - q

is comparable across tasks in the sense that the human reference is always at level q.

Remark 3.5. Without Axiom N, the quantity M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) inherits an arbitrary scaling from the choice of \phi_T, and \Theta_A is not well-defined as a cross-task coverage.

