4. The Dominance Survival Function

4.1. Measurability

Lemma 4.0 (Measurability of the margin field). Let T \mapsto s_A(T) and T \mapsto s_H^{(q)}(T) be \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable raw-score fields, and let \{\phi_T\} satisfy Axiom N. Then

T \mapsto M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) = \phi_T(s_A(T)) - q

is \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable.

Proof. The map T \mapsto s_A(T) is measurable by hypothesis. If \phi_T is measurable in T for each fixed argument (Assumption 4.0a below), then the composition T \mapsto \phi_T(s_A(T)) is measurable. Since q is a constant, the difference is measurable. \square

Assumption 4.0a (Joint measurability of normalization). The map (T, s) \mapsto \phi_T(s) is jointly measurable with respect to \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}} \otimes \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}).

This assumption is satisfied by all standard normalizations (rank, min-max, quantile-transformed).

4.2. The Margin Field

Definition 4.1 (Margin Field). Define

X_A = M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T), \qquad T \sim \mu.

By Lemma 4.0, X_A is a real-valued random variable on (\mathcal{T}, \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}, \mu).

4.3. The Dominance Survival Function

Definition 4.2 (Dominance Survival Function). For \lambda \in \mathbb{R}, define

\boxed{
\Theta_A(\lambda) = \mu\left(\left\{T \in \mathcal{T} : M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) \geq \lambda\right\}\right) = \Pr(X_A \geq \lambda).
}

The parameter \lambda represents the intensity; the value \Theta_A(\lambda) represents the extent.

4.4. Dominance Region and Quantile

Definition 4.3 (Dominance Region).

\mathcal{D}_A = \left\{(\lambda, \theta) : \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \theta\right\}.

Definition 4.4 (Dominance Quantile). For \theta \in (0,1]:

Q_A(\theta) = \sup\left\{\lambda \in \mathbb{R} : \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \theta\right\} = \Theta_A^{-1}(\theta).

4.5. Generality and Performance Depth

Definition 4.5 (Generality).

G_A = \Theta_A(0) = \mu\left(\left\{T : M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) \geq 0\right\}\right).

Definition 4.6 (Performance Depth).

P_A = \operatorname{ess\,sup}(X_A) = \sup\left\{\lambda \in \mathbb{R} : \Theta_A(\lambda) > 0\right\}.

Remark 4.1 (Non-attainment). The supremum P_A need not be attained. In general, \Theta_A(P_A) = 0, and the pair (G_A, P_A) does not correspond to a point in \mathcal{D}_A. Figure 1 illustrates a strictly decreasing \Theta_A with no atom at P_A.

Proposition 4.1. \mathcal{D}_A determines G_A and P_A.

Proposition 4.2 (Non-determination). (G_A, P_A) does not determine \mathcal{D}_A.

Proof. Construct A with \Theta_A(\lambda) = \mathbf{1}_{[-0.5, 0]}(\lambda) and B with \Theta_B(\lambda) = \mathbf{1}_{[0, 0.5]}(\lambda) \cdot \frac{1}{2} + \mathbf{1}_{[-0.5, 0)}(\lambda) \cdot \frac{1}{2}. Both have G = 1, P = 0.5, but \Theta_A \neq \Theta_B. \square

Corollary 4.1. \Theta_A is strictly more informative than (G_A, P_A).

4.6. The Fundamental Object

\boxed{
\Theta_A : \mathbb{R} \to [0, 1].
}

All scalar summaries of static dominance are derived functionals of \Theta_A.

