9. Reconstruction, Legg–Hutter Connection, and Empirical Instantiation

9.1. Reconstruction from Finite Samples

Problem 9.5 (Reconstruction). Let T_1, \ldots, T_n \sim \mu be i.i.d. samples. Define the empirical dominance survival function:

\widehat{\Theta}_A(\lambda) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \mathbf{1}_{\left\{M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T_i) \geq \lambda\right\}}.

Theorem 9.1 (DKW bound). If M_{A,H}^{(q)} is continuous, then for every \varepsilon > 0:

\Pr\left(\sup_{\lambda \in \mathbb{R}} \left|\widehat{\Theta}_A(\lambda) - \Theta_A(\lambda)\right| > \varepsilon\right) \leq 2 e^{-2n\varepsilon^2}.

Proof. The empirical CDF of a continuous random variable satisfies the Dvoretzky–Kiefer–Wolfowitz inequality. The survival function is 1 - F, so the bound transfers. \square

Corollary 9.1. With n \geq \frac{1}{2\varepsilon^2} \log\left(\frac{2}{\alpha}\right) samples, we have uniform estimation error \leq \varepsilon with probability 1 - \alpha.

For infinite task spaces, the function class \left\{\mathbf{1}_{M \geq \lambda} : \lambda \in \mathbb{R}\right\} is a VC class of dimension 1, so the uniform Glivenko–Cantelli property holds.

9.2. Relationship to Legg–Hutter

Problem 9.8. Suppose \mu_K(T) \propto 2^{-K(T)} is a complexity-weighted task measure. Then \Theta_A^K(\lambda) = \mu_K\left(\{M \geq \lambda\}\right) is the complexity-weighted dominance survival function.

Observation 9.1. High \Theta_A^K does not imply high \Upsilon(A), because \Upsilon averages value over environments, whereas \Theta_A^K measures human-relative margin coverage. The two objectives are formally distinct.

Observation 9.2. The construction \Theta_A^K provides a bridge: it inherits architecture-independence from the Kolmogorov prior while preserving the human-relative margin structure.

9.3. Empirical Instantiation Program

Plan 9.3. Three-domain instantiation:

1. Mathematical competition problems. \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{math}}: IMO-Shortlist-style problems. p(A, T) = fraction of correct solutions; p_H^{(q)}(T) = q-quantile of human competitor scores.
2. Code generation. \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{code}}: HumanEval-style tasks. p(A, T) = pass rate; p_H^{(q)}(T) = q-quantile of human programmer pass rates.
3. Natural language reasoning. \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{NL}}: MMLU-style questions. p(A, T) = accuracy; p_H^{(q)}(T) = q-quantile of human expert accuracy.

Protocol. For each domain, define \mu explicitly via a sampling distribution over subdomains with stated weights. Compute \widehat{\Theta}_A(\lambda) for frontier models. Report at q = 0.9. Compare across models via \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}}.

Falsifiability condition. If \widehat{\Theta}_A(\lambda) < \theta for the claimed (\lambda, \theta) after accounting for sampling error via Corollary 9.1, the superintelligence claim is refuted.

