10. Falsifiability and Open Problems

10.1. Falsifiability Levels

Level 1 — Empirical. Refutation by measurement error.

Level 2 — Structural. Refutation by empirical inseparability of dimensions.

Level 3 — Representation. Refutation by representation-dependence under admissible transformations.

Level 4 — Measure sensitivity. Refutation by reversal under admissible perturbation of \mu, unless \Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}} is adopted.

10.2. Open Problems

Problem 10.1 (Canonical cost). Is c in Definition 3.3 canonical in any sense? What reference class \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{ref}} is minimal?

Problem 10.2 (Canonical measure). Characterize the family \mathfrak{M} of admissible \mu.

Problem 10.3 (Sharpness of robust dominance). Characterize the maximal \varepsilon for which \Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon) > 0.

Problem 10.4 (Invariance). Characterize transformations \phi: \mathfrak{T} \to \mathfrak{T}' under which dominance classification is invariant.

Problem 10.5 (Reconstruction — deep version). For infinite task spaces with finite samples, characterize the minimax rate of estimation of \Theta_A.

Problem 10.6 (Minimality). Is the five-dimensional region \mathcal{F}_A minimal?

Problem 10.7 (Order structure). Does \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} admit a canonical completion?

Problem 10.8 (Legg–Hutter bridge). Under what conditions on \mu does \Theta_A^K dominate \Upsilon?

Problem 10.9 (Chollet relation). How does E_A(\eta) relate to Chollet's skill-acquisition efficiency?

Problem 10.10 (Empirical instantiation). Complete the three-domain program of Plan 9.3.

---

11. Conclusion

The principal results of this paper are:

First, the fundamental object is the dominance survival function \Theta_A(\lambda) = \mu(\{M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) \geq \lambda\}).

Second, the pair (G_A, P_A) are derived functionals of \Theta_A, and (G_A, P_A) does not determine \Theta_A (Proposition 4.2).

Third, no scalar order embedding of the product order into (\mathbb{R}, \leq) preserves all order relations (Theorem 5.1). The stochastic dominance relation is a preorder, becoming a partial order only on equivalence classes.

Fourth, robustness is formalized via optimal-transport ambiguity sets (Definition 7.1, Theorem 7.1).

Fifth, learning efficiency uses a logarithmic acquisition-time ratio (Definition 8.2); autonomy is retained as a graded profile (Definition 8.5), deliberately not scalarized.

Sixth, the dominance survival function is estimable from finite samples with DKW-uniform error bounds (Theorem 9.1).

Seventh, the collision audit confirms the specific construction has no direct precedent.

The framework does not claim to resolve the problem of superintelligence. It converts the problem into a form in which partial results can be stated rigorously, open problems can be formulated precisely, and empirical claims can be tested.

