Appendix A — Measurability and Technical Lemmas

A.1. Standing Assumptions

Throughout, let \mathfrak{T} = (\mathcal{T}, \tau_{\mathcal{T}}, \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}, \mu) be a structured task space as in Definition 3.2, with \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}} a \sigma-algebra containing the Borel \sigma-algebra generated by \tau_{\mathcal{T}}, and \mu a probability measure on (\mathcal{T}, \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}).

We assume the following measurability conditions.

(MA1) For each agent A, the raw-score field T \mapsto s_A(T) \in \mathbb{R} is \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable.

(MA2) For each quantile order q \in (0,1), the human reference field T \mapsto s_H^{(q)}(T) \in \mathbb{R} is \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable.

(MA3) The normalization family \{\phi_T\}_{T \in \mathcal{T}}, with \phi_T : \mathbb{R} \to [0,1], is jointly measurable with respect to \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}} \otimes \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}): the map (T, s) \mapsto \phi_T(s) is measurable.

(MA4) For every T \in \mathcal{T}, the map \phi_T is continuous and monotone (non-decreasing for higher-is-better tasks; non-increasing for lower-is-better tasks).

Remark A.1. Conditions MA1 and MA2 are satisfied in all standard benchmark settings: scores are computed by an evaluation protocol, and the human reference distribution is estimated from a finite sample. Condition MA3 is satisfied by rank normalization, min-max normalization, and quantile transformation. Condition MA4 is satisfied by all standard normalizations.

---

A.2. Lemma A.1 (Composition of measurable maps)

Lemma A.1. Let f : \mathcal{T} \to \mathbb{R} be \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable, and let \psi : \mathcal{T} \times \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} be jointly measurable. Then the map T \mapsto \psi(T, f(T)) is \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable.

Proof. The map \Phi : \mathcal{T} \to \mathcal{T} \times \mathbb{R} defined by \Phi(T) = (T, f(T)) is measurable with respect to \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}} and the product \sigma-algebra \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}} \otimes \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}), because the identity map T \mapsto T is \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable and f is \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable. The composition \psi \circ \Phi is therefore \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable as a composition of measurable maps. \square

---

A.3. Lemma A.2 (Continuity implies Borel measurability)

Lemma A.2. Let \phi_T : \mathbb{R} \to [0,1] be continuous for every T \in \mathcal{T}. Then for every B \in \mathcal{B}([0,1]), the set

\{(T, s) \in \mathcal{T} \times \mathbb{R} : \phi_T(s) \in B\}

is in \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}} \otimes \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}) provided that the family \{\phi_T\} is jointly measurable.

Proof. By Assumption MA3. \square

---

A.4. Lemma A.3 (Measurability of the margin field)

Lemma A.3. Under assumptions (MA1)–(MA4), the map

T \mapsto M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) = \phi_T(s_A(T)) - q

is \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable.

Proof. By Lemma A.1 applied to f = s_A (measurable by MA1) and \psi(T, s) = \phi_T(s) (jointly measurable by MA3), the composition T \mapsto \phi_T(s_A(T)) is \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable. Subtraction of the constant q \in \mathbb{R} preserves measurability. \square

---

A.5. Corollary A.4 (Measurability of superhuman sets)

Corollary A.4. For every \lambda \in \mathbb{R}, the superhuman set

S_\lambda(A; H_q) = \{T \in \mathcal{T} : M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) \geq \lambda\}

is \Sigma_{\mathcal{T}}-measurable.

Proof. The interval [\lambda, \infty) is Borel. The preimage under a measurable map is measurable. \square

---

A.6. Lemma A.5 (Monotonicity of the dominance survival function)

Lemma A.5. The map \lambda \mapsto \Theta_A(\lambda) is non-increasing, right-continuous, and satisfies \lim_{\lambda \to -\infty} \Theta_A(\lambda) = 1 and \lim_{\lambda \to +\infty} \Theta_A(\lambda) = 0.

Proof. For \lambda_1 \leq \lambda_2, we have S_{\lambda_2}(A; H_q) \subseteq S_{\lambda_1}(A; H_q), hence \Theta_A(\lambda_2) \leq \Theta_A(\lambda_1). Right-continuity follows from continuity of \mu from above and the identity

S_\lambda(A; H_q) = \bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} S_{\lambda - 1/n}(A; H_q).

The limit at -\infty follows from S_\lambda \uparrow \mathcal{T} as \lambda \to -\infty. The limit at +\infty follows from S_\lambda \downarrow \varnothing as \lambda \to +\infty, provided X_A is finite almost surely. \square

Remark A.2. If X_A has bounded support, then \Theta_A(\lambda) = 0 for all \lambda > \operatorname{ess\,sup} X_A. If X_A is unbounded above, then \Theta_A(\lambda) > 0 for all \lambda but \Theta_A(\lambda) \to 0.

---

Appendix B — Proofs for Section 4

B.1. Proof of Proposition 4.1

Proposition 4.1. The dominance region \mathcal{D}_A determines G_A and P_A uniquely.

Proof. We recover G_A and P_A from \mathcal{D}_A as follows.

By Definition 4.5,

G_A = \Theta_A(0) = \sup\{\theta : (0, \theta) \in \mathcal{D}_A\}.

Since \mathcal{D}_A = \{(\lambda, \theta) : \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \theta\}, the vertical slice at \lambda = 0 is [0, \Theta_A(0)], so G_A is recovered as the supremum of \theta in this slice.

By Definition 4.6,

P_A = \sup\{\lambda : \Theta_A(\lambda) > 0\} = \sup\{\lambda : (\lambda, 0^+) \in \mathcal{D}_A\}.

Equivalently, P_A is the supremum of \lambda such that the horizontal slice \{\theta : (\lambda, \theta) \in \mathcal{D}_A\} is non-trivial. \square

---

B.2. Proof of Proposition 4.2

Proposition 4.2. The pair (G_A, P_A) does not determine \mathcal{D}_A.

Proof. We construct two agents A, B with the same G and P but different dominance survival functions.

Let \mathcal{T} = [0, 1] with Lebesgue measure \mu, and let the margin fields be:

M_{A}(T) = \begin{cases} -0.5 & T \in [0, 0.5), \\ 0.5 & T \in [0.5, 1]. \end{cases}

M_{B}(T) = \begin{cases} -0.5 & T \in [0, 0.25), \\ 0.25 & T \in [0.25, 0.75), \\ 0.5 & T \in [0.75, 1]. \end{cases}

Compute \Theta_A:

\Theta_A(\lambda) = \begin{cases} 1 & \lambda \leq -0.5, \\ 0.5 & -0.5 < \lambda \leq 0.5, \\ 0 & \lambda > 0.5. \end{cases}

Compute \Theta_B:

\Theta_B(\lambda) = \begin{cases} 1 & \lambda \leq -0.5, \\ 0.75 & -0.5 < \lambda \leq 0.25, \\ 0.25 & 0.25 < \lambda \leq 0.5, \\ 0 & \lambda > 0.5. \end{cases}

Compare:

· G_A = \Theta_A(0) = 0.5, G_B = \Theta_B(0) = 0.75. These differ.

We adjust the construction to equalize G. Set:

M_{A}(T) = \begin{cases} -0.5 & T \in [0, 0.5), \\ 0.5 & T \in [0.5, 1]. \end{cases}

M_{B}(T) = \begin{cases} -1.0 & T \in [0, 0.5), \\ 0.5 & T \in [0.5, 1]. \end{cases}

Now:

· G_A = \Theta_A(0) = 0.5, G_B = \Theta_B(0) = 0.5.
· P_A = 0.5, P_B = 0.5.

But \Theta_A \neq \Theta_B: for \lambda \in (-0.5, 0], \Theta_A(\lambda) = 0.5, while \Theta_B(\lambda) = 0. For \lambda \in (-1, -0.5], \Theta_A(\lambda) = 0.5 if \lambda > -0.5, while \Theta_B(\lambda) = 1 if \lambda \leq -1.

Hence \mathcal{D}_A \neq \mathcal{D}_B despite (G_A, P_A) = (G_B, P_B). \square

Remark B.1. A more visual construction: consider two probability distributions on \mathbb{R} with the same mean and the same support, but different shapes (e.g., a point mass vs. a uniform distribution). Their survival functions differ while their extremes coincide.

---

B.3. Corollary B.2.1 (Non-attainment of P_A)

Corollary B.2.1. It is possible that \Theta_A(P_A) = 0, in which case P_A \notin \operatorname{proj}_\lambda(\mathcal{D}_A).

Proof. Consider a margin field M_A(T) = 1 - T for T \in (0, 1] with \mu the Lebesgue measure on (0,1]. Then

\Theta_A(\lambda) = \mu(\{T : 1 - T \geq \lambda\}) = \mu(\{T : T \leq 1 - \lambda\}) = 1 - \lambda

for \lambda \in [0, 1], and \Theta_A(\lambda) = 0 for \lambda > 1. Then P_A = 1 but \Theta_A(1) = 0. \square

