Appendix C — Proofs for Section 5

C.1. Proof of Theorem 5.1

Theorem 5.1. There is no function f : \mathbb{R}^5 \to \mathbb{R} that is simultaneously injective, order-preserving, and order-reflecting with respect to the product order \preceq.

Proof. Suppose such f exists. Consider the points

x = (1, 0, 0, 0, 0), \qquad y = (0, 1, 0, 0, 0).

They are incomparable under \preceq: neither x \preceq y nor y \preceq x holds.

Since f(x), f(y) \in \mathbb{R} and \mathbb{R} is totally ordered, either f(x) \leq f(y) or f(y) \leq f(x).

Case 1: f(x) \leq f(y). By order-reflection, x \preceq y, contradicting incomparability.

Case 2: f(y) \leq f(x). By order-reflection, y \preceq x, contradicting incomparability.

Both cases yield contradictions. Therefore no such f exists. \square

Remark C.1. The proof does not require injectivity. The theorem is stronger: no order-preserving, order-reflecting map into a totally ordered set exists.

---

C.2. Proof of Proposition 5.1

Proposition 5.1. The relation \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} on agents is a preorder. It becomes a partial order on the quotient space of equivalence classes under the relation

A \sim B \iff \Theta_A(\lambda) = \Theta_B(\lambda) \ \forall \lambda.

Proof.

Reflexivity. For any agent A, \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \Theta_A(\lambda) for all \lambda, so A \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} A.

Transitivity. If A \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} B and B \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} C, then for all \lambda, \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \Theta_B(\lambda) \geq \Theta_C(\lambda), hence A \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} C.

Thus \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} is a preorder.

Antisymmetry on quotient. Suppose A \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} B and B \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} A. Then \Theta_A(\lambda) = \Theta_B(\lambda) for all \lambda, so A \sim B. On the quotient space \mathcal{A}/{\sim}, the relation is antisymmetric: if [A] \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} [B] and [B] \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} [A], then [A] = [B]. \square

---

C.3. Proposition C.3.1 (Strict dominance characterization)

Proposition C.3.1. A \succ_{\mathrm{SD}} B (strict) if and only if \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \Theta_B(\lambda) for all \lambda and \Theta_A(\lambda_0) > \Theta_B(\lambda_0) for some \lambda_0.

Proof. Immediate from the definitions. \square

