Appendix D — Proofs for Section 7 (Robustness)

D.1. Wasserstein Distance — Standing Assumptions

Assumption D.1. The task cost c : \mathcal{T} \times \mathcal{T} \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} satisfies:

(i) c is symmetric: c(T, T') = c(T', T);

(ii) c is lower semi-continuous with respect to \tau_{\mathcal{T}} \otimes \tau_{\mathcal{T}};

(iii) c(T, T) = 0 for all T, and c(T, T') > 0 for T \neq T';

(iv) c is bounded: \sup_{T, T'} c(T, T') < \infty.

Definition D.1 (Wasserstein distance). For \nu, \mu \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{T}):

W_c(\nu, \mu) = \inf_{\gamma \in \Gamma(\nu, \mu)} \int_{\mathcal{T} \times \mathcal{T}} c(T, T') \, d\gamma(T, T'),

where \Gamma(\nu, \mu) is the set of couplings with marginals \nu and \mu.

Proposition D.1. Under Assumption D.1, W_c is a metric on \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{T}).

Proof. Symmetry, non-negativity, and the triangle inequality follow from standard optimal-transport theory (Villani, 2009, Chapter 6). The identity of indiscernibles holds because c(T, T') > 0 for T \neq T' and c is bounded. \square

---

D.2. Proof of Theorem 7.1

Theorem 7.1. The robust dominance survival function

\Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon) = \inf_{\nu \in \mathfrak{U}_\varepsilon(\mu)} \nu(S_\lambda(A; H_q))

is non-increasing in \varepsilon.

Proof. Let \varepsilon_1 \leq \varepsilon_2. Then

\mathfrak{U}_{\varepsilon_1}(\mu) = \{\nu : W_c(\nu, \mu) \leq \varepsilon_1\} \subseteq \{\nu : W_c(\nu, \mu) \leq \varepsilon_2\} = \mathfrak{U}_{\varepsilon_2}(\mu).

Since \mathfrak{U}_{\varepsilon_1} \subseteq \mathfrak{U}_{\varepsilon_2}, the infimum over the larger set is no larger:

\inf_{\nu \in \mathfrak{U}_{\varepsilon_2}} \nu(S_\lambda) \leq \inf_{\nu \in \mathfrak{U}_{\varepsilon_1}} \nu(S_\lambda).

Therefore \Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon_2) \leq \Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon_1). \square

---

D.3. Proposition D.3.1 (Continuity of the robust survival function)

Proposition D.3.1. Under Assumption D.1, the map \varepsilon \mapsto \Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon) is continuous from the right.

Proof. The function \nu \mapsto \nu(S_\lambda) is W_c-Lipschitz with constant L = \sup_{T, T'} c(T, T') when S_\lambda is measurable (by Kantorovich duality and the boundedness of c). More precisely, for any two measures \nu_1, \nu_2,

|\nu_1(S_\lambda) - \nu_2(S_\lambda)| \leq L \cdot W_c(\nu_1, \nu_2).

The infimum of a Lipschitz function over a closed ball is attained and varies continuously with the radius. Right-continuity follows from the closedness of \mathfrak{U}_\varepsilon(\mu) and the compactness of \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{T}) under W_c. \square

---

D.4. Proposition D.4.1 (Robust region is contained in nominal region)

Proposition D.4.1. \mathcal{D}_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\varepsilon) \subseteq \mathcal{D}_A(0).

Proof. For any \varepsilon \geq 0, \mu \in \mathfrak{U}_\varepsilon(\mu), so

\Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon) = \inf_{\nu \in \mathfrak{U}_\varepsilon(\mu)} \nu(S_\lambda) \leq \mu(S_\lambda) = \Theta_A(\lambda).

Thus if \Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\lambda, \varepsilon) \geq \theta, then \Theta_A(\lambda) \geq \theta, so (\lambda, \theta) \in \mathcal{D}_A(0). \square

---

Appendix E — Proofs for Section 9 (Reconstruction)

E.1. The DKW Inequality

Theorem E.1 (Dvoretzky–Kiefer–Wolfowitz, 1956; Massart, 1990). Let X_1, \ldots, X_n \sim F be i.i.d. real random variables with CDF F. Let

\widehat{F}_n(t) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \mathbf{1}_{\{X_i \leq t\}}

be the empirical CDF. Then for every \varepsilon > 0:

\Pr\left(\sup_{t \in \mathbb{R}} \left|\widehat{F}_n(t) - F(t)\right| > \varepsilon\right) \leq 2 \exp(-2n\varepsilon^2).

The constant 2 is sharp (Massart, 1990).

---

E.2. Proof of Theorem 9.1

Theorem 9.1. Under the assumptions of Section A.1, let T_1, \ldots, T_n \sim \mu be i.i.d. task samples. Define

\widehat{\Theta}_A(\lambda) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \mathbf{1}_{\{M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T_i) \geq \lambda\}}.

Then for every \varepsilon > 0:

\Pr\left(\sup_{\lambda \in \mathbb{R}} \left|\widehat{\Theta}_A(\lambda) - \Theta_A(\lambda)\right| > \varepsilon\right) \leq 2 \exp(-2n\varepsilon^2).

Proof. The dominance survival function \Theta_A(\lambda) = \Pr(X_A \geq \lambda) is the survival function of the random variable X_A = M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) with T \sim \mu. The empirical version \widehat{\Theta}_A is the empirical survival function.

Let F_A(\lambda) = \Pr(X_A \leq \lambda) = 1 - \Theta_A(\lambda). Let \widehat{F}_A(\lambda) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_i \mathbf{1}_{\{X_{A,i} \leq \lambda\}} where X_{A,i} = M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T_i).

Since \widehat{\Theta}_A = 1 - \widehat{F}_A and \Theta_A = 1 - F_A, we have

\sup_{\lambda} |\widehat{\Theta}_A(\lambda) - \Theta_A(\lambda)| = \sup_{\lambda} |\widehat{F}_A(\lambda) - F_A(\lambda)|.

Applying Theorem E.1 to the i.i.d. sequence X_{A,1}, \ldots, X_{A,n}:

\Pr\left(\sup_{\lambda} |\widehat{F}_A(\lambda) - F_A(\lambda)| > \varepsilon\right) \leq 2 \exp(-2n\varepsilon^2).

The result follows. \square

Remark E.1 (Continuity not required). The DKW inequality holds for arbitrary CDFs, without continuity assumptions.

---

E.3. Corollary E.3.1 (Sample complexity)

Corollary E.3.1. To achieve uniform estimation error \leq \varepsilon with confidence 1 - \alpha, it suffices to take

n \geq \frac{1}{2\varepsilon^2} \log\left(\frac{2}{\alpha}\right).

Proof. Set the right-hand side of the DKW bound equal to \alpha:

2 \exp(-2n\varepsilon^2) = \alpha
\implies -2n\varepsilon^2 = \log(\alpha/2)
\implies n = \frac{1}{2\varepsilon^2} \log(2/\alpha).

\square

---

E.4. Uniform Glivenko–Cantelli for the Survival Function Class

Definition E.1. Let \mathcal{F} = \{\mathbf{1}_{[\lambda, \infty)} : \lambda \in \mathbb{R}\} be the class of indicator functions of half-lines.

Proposition E.4.1. The class \mathcal{F} is a VC class of dimension 1.

Proof. The VC dimension of the set of half-lines in \mathbb{R} is 1. Specifically:

· Any single point can be shattered (choose a half-line containing it or not).
· No two distinct points x_1 < x_2 can be shattered: the labeling x_1 \mapsto 0, x_2 \mapsto 1 is not achievable by any half-line [\lambda, \infty) (if x_2 \geq \lambda, then x_1 < x_2 implies x_1 \geq \lambda only if \lambda \leq x_1, contradiction).

Therefore VCdim(\mathcal{F}) = 1. \square

Corollary E.4.2. The class \mathcal{F} is a uniform Glivenko–Cantelli class, so

\sup_{\lambda} |\widehat{\Theta}_A(\lambda) - \Theta_A(\lambda)| \to 0

almost surely as n \to \infty.

---

E.5. Relationship to Legg–Hutter: Formal Statement

Proposition E.5.1. Let \mu_K(T) \propto 2^{-K(T)} be a complexity-weighted task measure. Define

\Theta_A^K(\lambda) = \mu_K(\{T : M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) \geq \lambda\}).

Then \Theta_A^K is not in general a monotone functional of \Upsilon(A).

Proof. By construction, \Theta_A^K weights tasks by complexity. \Upsilon weights environments by complexity and integrates value. Two agents with the same value profile on simple environments but different value profiles on complex environments can have the same \Upsilon but different \Theta_A^K. Conversely, two agents with the same \Theta_A^K can have different \Upsilon if their value profiles differ on complex environments.

Formally, let \mathcal{A}_1, \mathcal{A}_2 be agents. If \Upsilon(\mathcal{A}_1) = \Upsilon(\mathcal{A}_2) but their margin distributions differ, then \Theta_{\mathcal{A}_1}^K \neq \Theta_{\mathcal{A}_2}^K in general. \square

Remark E.2. A formal bridge requires additional assumptions on the relationship between value and margin, which is outside the scope of this paper. This is Problem 10.8.

