Appendix F — Details on Task Cost Construction

F.1. Composite Cost Structure

Recall Definition 3.3:

c(T, T') = \alpha \cdot d_{\mathcal{X}}(T, T') + \beta \cdot d_{\mathcal{Y}}(T, T') + \gamma \cdot d_{\mathcal{R}}(T, T').

We now specify each component.

---

F.2. Observation Distance d_{\mathcal{X}}

Definition F.1. Let \mathcal{X}(T) denote the observation space of task T. We assume a family of embeddings

e_{\mathcal{X}} : \bigcup_{T \in \mathcal{T}} \mathcal{X}(T) \to \mathbb{R}^d

into a common finite-dimensional space. Define

d_{\mathcal{X}}(T, T') = W_2\left(e_{\mathcal{X} \#} \mu_T^{\mathcal{X}}, \; e_{\mathcal{X} \#} \mu_{T'}^{\mathcal{X}}\right),

where \mu_T^{\mathcal{X}} is the distribution of observations under task T, e_{\mathcal{X} \#} denotes the pushforward, and W_2 is the 2-Wasserstein distance on \mathbb{R}^d with the Euclidean cost.

Remark F.1. The embedding e_{\mathcal{X}} may be obtained from a pretrained representation model. This makes d_{\mathcal{X}} architecture-dependent. The dependence is explicit.

---

F.3. Action Distance d_{\mathcal{Y}}

Definition F.2. Let \mathcal{Y}(T) denote the action space of task T. Assume a family of embeddings

e_{\mathcal{Y}} : \bigcup_{T \in \mathcal{T}} \mathcal{Y}(T) \to \mathbb{R}^d.

Define

d_{\mathcal{Y}}(T, T') = W_2\left(e_{\mathcal{Y} \#} \pi^*_T, \; e_{\mathcal{Y} \#} \pi^*_{T'}\right),

where \pi^*_T is the optimal action distribution under task T for a fixed reference agent class.

---

F.4. Reward Distance d_{\mathcal{R}}

Definition F.3. Let \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{ref}} be a fixed reference class of agents. For each A \in \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{ref}}, let \pi^*_A(T) denote the optimal policy of A on task T. Define

d_{\mathcal{R}}(T, T') = \sup_{A \in \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{ref}}} \left\|\pi^*_A(T) - \pi^*_A(T')\right\|_{\mathrm{TV}},

where \|\cdot\|_{\mathrm{TV}} is the total variation norm on policy space.

---

F.5. Consistency of the Composite Cost

Proposition F.5.1. If each of d_{\mathcal{X}}, d_{\mathcal{Y}}, d_{\mathcal{R}} is a pseudo-metric, then c is a pseudo-metric.

Proof. Symmetry: each component is symmetric, so c is symmetric. Triangle inequality: each component satisfies the triangle inequality, and non-negative linear combinations of pseudo-metrics satisfy the triangle inequality. c(T, T) = 0 follows from each component vanishing on the diagonal. \square

Proposition F.5.2. Under Assumption D.1 (boundedness), c is bounded.

Proof. Each component is bounded by construction (the embeddings are into bounded spaces, and the total variation norm is bounded by 2). \square

---

F.6. Sensitivity to Weights (\alpha, \beta, \gamma)

Problem F.1. The weights \alpha, \beta, \gamma in Definition 3.3 are not canonically determined.

Proposition F.6.1. If the robust dominance region \mathcal{D}_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(\varepsilon) is invariant under changes of (\alpha, \beta, \gamma) within a range, then the robustness analysis is stable.

Proof. The robust region is defined via the ambiguity set \mathfrak{U}_\varepsilon(\mu), which depends on c through W_c. If c changes by a bounded factor, the ambiguity set changes by a corresponding factor, and the robust region may change. Stability requires the change to be small relative to \varepsilon. This is a continuity property of the robust dominance survival function in c, which we leave as an open problem. \square

---

Appendix G — Details on the Normalization Axiom

G.1. Proof of the Compatibility Property

Proposition G.1. Under Axiom N, the normalized human reference satisfies p_H^{(q)}(T) = q.

Proof. By Axiom N2, \phi_T(s_H^{(q)}) = q. Since p_H^{(q)}(T) = \phi_T(s_H^{(q)}) by Definition 3.4 and Axiom N, we obtain p_H^{(q)}(T) = q. \square

---

G.2. Canonical Examples

Example G.1 (Rank normalization). Let F_T be the CDF of human scores on T. Define

\phi_T(s) = F_T(s).

Then \phi_T(s_H^{(q)}) = F_T(F_T^{-1}(q)) = q if F_T is continuous and strictly increasing.

Example G.2 (Min-max normalization). Let m_T = \min_T s, M_T = \max_T s. Define

\phi_T(s) = \frac{s - m_T}{M_T - m_T}.

Then \phi_T(s_H^{(q)}) = q iff s_H^{(q)} = m_T + q(M_T - m_T), which is the case for uniform distributions.

Example G.3 (Quantile transformation). Define

\phi_T(s) = F_T(s)

where F_T is the empirical CDF. This is the standard approach in practice.

---

G.3. The Necessity of Axiom N

Proposition G.3. Without Axiom N, the dominance survival function \Theta_A(\lambda) is not invariant under admissible rescaling of raw scores.

Proof. Consider two normalizations \phi_T and \psi_T = \phi_T + c for a constant c. Then M_{A,H}^{(q), \psi}(T) = M_{A,H}^{(q), \phi}(T) + c. The corresponding survival functions are related by \Theta_A^\psi(\lambda) = \Theta_A^\phi(\lambda - c). Thus the shape of \Theta_A is translation-invariant but the threshold \lambda is shifted.

If instead \psi_T = a \phi_T with a \neq 1, then \Theta_A^\psi(\lambda) = \Theta_A^\phi(\lambda / a), which rescales the horizontal axis. Axiom N prevents arbitrary rescaling by pinning p_H^{(q)}(T) = q. \square

---

Appendix H — Details on the Autonomy Profile

H.1. Proof of the Non-Scalarization Argument

Proposition H.1. Any scalarization A_A = \sum_k w_k A_k with w_k > 0 introduces an ordering principle that is not determined by the autonomy profile \mathbf{A}_A.

Proof. Two profiles \mathbf{A} = (1, 0, 0, 0) and \mathbf{B} = (0, 1, 0, 0) are incomparable under the product order on \mathbb{R}^4. A scalarization with weights w_0 > w_1 ranks \mathbf{A} \succ \mathbf{B}; a scalarization with w_1 > w_0 reverses the ranking. Therefore the scalar ranking depends on the arbitrary choice of weights. \square

---

H.2. Autonomy-Constrained Dominance

Definition H.1. The autonomy-constrained dominance survival function at level k is

\Theta_A^{\mathrm{auto}}(\lambda, k) = \mu\left(\left\{T : M_{A,H}^{(q)}(T) \geq \lambda \text{ and } \mathcal{A}(T) \geq k\right\}\right).

Proposition H.2. \Theta_A^{\mathrm{auto}}(\lambda, k) is non-increasing in k.

Proof. If k_1 \leq k_2, then \{T : \mathcal{A}(T) \geq k_2\} \subseteq \{T : \mathcal{A}(T) \geq k_1\}, so the intersection with S_\lambda is smaller, and the measure is no larger. \square

Proposition H.3. \Theta_A^{\mathrm{auto}}(\lambda, 0) = \Theta_A(\lambda).

Proof. Since \mathcal{A}(T) \geq 0 for all T by definition of the autonomy axis, the constraint is vacuous. \square

---

H.3. Multi-Axis Autonomy

Definition H.2. Let \mathcal{A}_1, \ldots, \mathcal{A}_m be m distinct autonomy axes. The multi-axis autonomy profile is

\mathbf{A}_A = \left(A_{j,k}\right)_{j \in [m], k \in \{0,1,2,3\}}, \qquad A_{j,k} = \mu(\{T : \mathcal{A}_j(T) \geq k\}).

Remark H.1. The profile \mathbf{A}_A is a matrix of size m \times 4. Any scalarization requires both axis weights and level weights, doubling the arbitrariness. We therefore retain the profile.

