Appendix L — Numerical Example (Illustrative)

To make the framework concrete, we provide a minimal numerical illustration.

L.1. Setup

Let \mathcal{T} = \{T_1, T_2, T_3, T_4\} with uniform measure \mu(T_i) = 1/4. Let the normalized margins (under Axiom N) be:

Task M_{A}(T_i) M_{B}(T_i)
T_1 0.30 0.10
T_2 0.20 0.15
T_3 0.10 0.40
T_4 -0.10 0.35

L.2. Compute \Theta_A and \Theta_B

For agent A: sorted margins: -0.10, 0.10, 0.20, 0.30.

\Theta_A(\lambda) = \begin{cases} 1 & \lambda \leq -0.10, \\ 3/4 & -0.10 < \lambda \leq 0.10, \\ 2/4 & 0.10 < \lambda \leq 0.20, \\ 1/4 & 0.20 < \lambda \leq 0.30, \\ 0 & \lambda > 0.30. \end{cases}

For agent B: sorted margins: 0.10, 0.15, 0.35, 0.40.

\Theta_B(\lambda) = \begin{cases} 1 & \lambda \leq 0.10, \\ 3/4 & 0.10 < \lambda \leq 0.15, \\ 2/4 & 0.15 < \lambda \leq 0.35, \\ 1/4 & 0.35 < \lambda \leq 0.40, \\ 0 & \lambda > 0.40. \end{cases}

L.3. Compare

· G_A = \Theta_A(0) = 3/4, G_B = \Theta_B(0) = 1.
· P_A = 0.30, P_B = 0.40.

Dominance relation: Compare \Theta_A and \Theta_B pointwise:

· At \lambda = 0.05: \Theta_A = 3/4, \Theta_B = 1. B dominates.
· At \lambda = 0.25: \Theta_A = 1/4, \Theta_B = 2/4. B dominates.
· At \lambda = -0.15: \Theta_A = 1, \Theta_B = 1.

In this example, \Theta_B(\lambda) \geq \Theta_A(\lambda) for all \lambda, so B \succeq_{\mathrm{SD}} A.

L.4. Robustness Illustration

Suppose \mathfrak{U}_\varepsilon(\mu) is a Wasserstein ball of radius \varepsilon = 0.1 around the uniform measure. The worst-case measure might concentrate mass on the task where A performs worst.

Worst-case for A: If \nu concentrates all mass on T_4 (where M_A = -0.10), then

\Theta_A^{\mathrm{rob}}(0, \varepsilon) = \nu(M_A \geq 0) = 0.

Worst-case for B: If \nu concentrates all mass on T_1 (where M_B = 0.10), then

\Theta_B^{\mathrm{rob}}(0, \varepsilon) = \nu(M_B \geq 0) = 1.

Thus B is robustly dominant at \lambda = 0, while A is not.

