Appendix M — Bibliographic Notes on the Collision Audit

M.1. Dolan & Moré (2002) — Performance Profiles

The performance profile \rho_s(\tau) is defined in the context of numerical optimization benchmarking. It is a survival function of the ratio t_{p,s} / \min_{s'} t_{p,s'}, aggregated across problems p. The domain is a finite set of test problems, not a measurable cognitive task space. The reference is the best solver, not a human population.

M.2. Fonseca et al. (2011) — Empirical Attainment Functions

The EAF \alpha_T(z) is defined over the objective space of a multiobjective optimization problem. It measures the fraction of algorithm runs that attain a target vector z. The domain is a continuous objective space, not a cognitive task space. The reference is the set of algorithm runs, not a human population.

M.3. Nature (2026) — Task-Level Stochastic Dominance

The study compares AI and human accuracy distributions on a single emotion recognition task. The stochastic dominance test is at the level of score distributions on that task. There is no task measure \mu, no margin field M_{A,H}(T), and no dominance survival function over a structured task space.

M.4. Cho et al. (2026) — Task-Space Complexity

The framework formalizes performance-based directional task dissimilarity and \varepsilon-tolerance sets for zero-shot generalization. The objective is generalization in reinforcement learning, not superintelligence. The reference is not a human population.

M.5. Ko (2019) — Multidimensional Superintelligence

Ko represents intelligent systems as regions in a multidimensional cognitive space and characterizes superintelligence geometrically. This is a direct precedent for multidimensional representation. The present work does not claim multidimensionality as novel.

M.6. Verdict

The collision audit confirms that:

\boxed{
\text{The pattern of survival/coverage functions over task collections is established.}
}

\boxed{
\text{The specific construction of a human-relative margin distribution over a measurable task space has no direct precedent.}
}

